### The seal's derivation

`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:300-303`:
```clojure
(defn- seal-of [secret kind id]
  (jing/content-hash [:yin.k/seal secret kind id] {:algorithm :blake3}))
```

A blake3 content-hash of the canonical CBOR bytes of the *vector* `[:yin.k/seal secret kind id]`. Its three inputs:

1. **`secret`** = `:capability-secret`. Random in *production* (`yin/repl.cljc:672`, `(str (random-uuid))`) but fixed per task. In the **test harness** it is the deterministic constant `"yin.vm.test-utils/capability-secret"` (`test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:33-38`), injected identically into both lanes (`tu/create-vm` merge + explicit `:capability-secret tu/secret` at `test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2580-2582`).
2. **`kind`** = a literal keyword.
3. **`id`** = `(keyword (str prefix "-" counter))` from a monotone `:id-counter` (`gensym`, `engine.cljc:2392-2398`).

I computed the seal three times against the test secret — identical every run, length 64 (`8492a3c7…`), matching the `str-length == 64` pre-filter in `condition-retained-form` (`yang/clojure.cljc:956`). The input is a vector, so there's no map-key ordering to diverge. None of the candidates in your hypothesis (object identity, RNG, `System/identityHashCode`, a gensym counter) feed the seal.

### Why the symptom can't arise in these tests

`:dead-or-forged-reference` means the cell id is absent from `:heap` (`check-ref!`, `engine.cljc:368-386`). But the heap is never swept here: `default-gc-threshold` is 4096 allocations (`yin/vm.cljc:1954-1958`), each test mints a fresh VM with a handful of cells, so GC never runs. The mint is self-consistent (`:cell/new-retained`, `engine.cljc:2826-2831`, gensyms the id, seals the ref, writes the entry with that ref's seal). A minted wrapper can only read back dead-or-forged if its entry is gone — which GC cannot have done.

